I agree with most of the advice you offered.
> 
> Don't read any unidentified scroll or quaff any unknown potion.

If you can price ID to a group where you are prepared for
any bad result you might chose to break that general rule. 
> 
> You can put on non-cursed rings, but take them off immediately if they
> don't auto-id.

I see this a lot. What are we scared of here, polymorph? If
your body armor and cloak aren't particularly special, say
mithril and a elven cloak, and you have enough HP (31) so
system shock won't kill you, why not. Yes, it can be
dangerous if you poly into something so small your armor
falls off, but chances are you can kill yourself and reclaim
your inventory before anything can kill your restored
character. if not, you probably weren't prepared for your
depth anyway. 

You want to rule out conflict before you walk into a store
but some very handy rings for early in the game can be IDed
if you wear them a while.

> Be devastatingly overprepared before attempting the quest, or Medusa's
> level, or the castle, or Ludios.

One way to be prepared is to have a well thought out plan.
How can I shape the battle to my favor. For example, if you
find a dragon in the mines, the reward is worth some
additional risk, but you should have prepared to somehow
disable the dragon, deal with the breath until you get close
and escape if things don't go well.

Then you need to find that poly trap and push a boulder over
it!
> 
> Be willing to go back and clear out all the vaults once you get a
> pickaxe; at the very least you can use that $ for protection.

Be willing to go back is more general. There's a big
advantage to be had from doing your housekeeping, once you
have the food situation well in hand. Going back to
consolidate stashes, price ID, sacrifice away your prayer
time-out, buy protection, etc.

> It's like playing blackjack with a single deck; if you count cards and
> play smart, you can put the odds in your favor.  But gamble even once
> in a while, and you're going to lose.

Well said!
